1
00:00:11,830 --> 00:00:15,510
 Hey guys, welcome back to our IELTS listening course.

2
00:00:15,930 --> 00:00:22,370
Today we're diving deep into Section 3, the section
that many students find the most intimidating.

3
00:00:22,590 --> 00:00:27,570
But here's the good news, once you understand how this section works and what

4
00:00:27,570 --> 00:00:30,470
to listen for, it becomes much more manageable.

5
00:00:30,470 --> 00:00:36,850
In this lesson, we will cover Format and Common Scenarios of Section 3,

6
00:00:37,610 --> 00:00:42,370
Difficulties of Section 3, Strategies for Section 3.

7
00:00:43,010 --> 00:00:48,730
By the end of this lesson, you'll understand exactly what makes Part 3 unique,

8
00:00:49,110 --> 00:00:56,830
why it's challenging, and most importantly, how to approach it strategically.

9
00:00:57,750 --> 00:00:58,110
Ready?

10
00:00:58,910 --> 00:00:59,770
Let's get started.

11
00:01:04,480 --> 00:01:05,900
Let's start with the basics.

12
00:01:06,180 --> 00:01:13,060
The IELTS listening test has four parts, and
Section 3 is the third section you'll encounter.

13
00:01:13,360 --> 00:01:15,480
But what makes it different from the others?

14
00:01:15,800 --> 00:01:20,200
In Sections 1 and 2, you're in everyday social situations.

15
00:01:20,580 --> 00:01:27,440
Section 1 is a conversation between two people, maybe
someone booking a hotel room or asking for directions.

16
00:01:28,120 --> 00:01:34,580
Section 2 is a monologue about a general
topic, like a tour guide describing a museum.

17
00:01:35,080 --> 00:01:38,500
But Section 3 is about an academic context.

18
00:01:38,800 --> 00:01:40,960
Here are the key features of Section 3.

19
00:01:41,220 --> 00:01:47,280
It is usually a conversation in an academic or
educational setting between two to three speakers.

20
00:01:47,540 --> 00:01:54,012
Unlike Section 4, which is also academic but features
just one lecturer speaking, Section 3 involves

21
00:01:54,036 --> 00:02:00,180
multiple people exchanging ideas, agreeing,
disagreeing, and building on each other's points.

22
00:02:01,380 --> 00:02:04,820
So, what kind of conversations will you hear?

23
00:02:05,020 --> 00:02:09,600
There are some common scenarios that you might listen for in Section 3.

24
00:02:09,880 --> 00:02:14,500
First scenario, two or three students discussing a group project or assignment.

25
00:02:14,740 --> 00:02:17,342
Maybe they're planning their research, dividing tasks,

26
00:02:17,366 --> 00:02:19,740
 or reviewing what they've learned.

27
00:02:19,740 --> 00:02:23,700
Second scenario, a student meeting with their tutor or professor.

28
00:02:24,040 --> 00:02:28,260
They might be discussing feedback on an essay, getting advice about a research topic,

29
00:02:28,260 --> 00:02:30,240
 or planning their dissertation.

30
00:02:30,620 --> 00:02:38,300
Third scenario, a seminar discussion where a tutor and students explore an

31
00:02:38,300 --> 00:02:45,300
academic topic together, perhaps debating
different theories or analyzing a case study.

32
00:02:45,500 --> 00:02:50,800
For the topic of this lesson, law and governance, you might hear students

33
00:02:50,800 --> 00:02:56,320
discussing legal case studies, comparing
different policy reforms with their professor.

34
00:03:01,920 --> 00:03:08,820
Many students find Section 3 particularly difficult,
and there are very specific reasons for this.

35
00:03:09,080 --> 00:03:11,440
Let me break down the five main challenges.

36
00:03:11,780 --> 00:03:14,720
The first challenge is tracking multiple speakers.

37
00:03:14,720 --> 00:03:19,420
Unlike Sections 1 and 2, where you're mostly following one or two voices,

38
00:03:20,120 --> 00:03:23,280
Section 3 can have up to four different people talking.

39
00:03:23,560 --> 00:03:24,560
Why is this hard?

40
00:03:24,840 --> 00:03:30,020
Each speaker may have different accents,
speaking speeds, and communication styles.

41
00:03:30,320 --> 00:03:34,600
Sometimes, speakers interrupt each other or finish each other's sentences.

42
00:03:35,080 --> 00:03:35,940
And here's the tricky part.

43
00:03:36,100 --> 00:03:39,300
The answer you need might come from a speaker you weren't focusing on, so

44
00:03:39,300 --> 00:03:42,440
 you should always stay alert to all speakers in Section 3.

45
00:03:42,960 --> 00:03:46,880
The second challenge is the complexity of ideas.

46
00:03:47,240 --> 00:03:52,320
While Sections 1 and 2 use more conversational, simple language in daily

47
00:03:52,320 --> 00:03:58,140
contexts, Section 3 deals with opinions,
theories, comparisons, and evaluations.

48
00:03:58,580 --> 00:04:04,440
The ideas are more abstract, and the language
used to express them is more sophisticated.

49
00:04:05,380 --> 00:04:10,320
Here's challenge number three, and this one catches many students.

50
00:04:10,320 --> 00:04:15,880
In academic discussions, people often change their minds, express uncertainty,

51
00:04:16,260 --> 00:04:18,300
or partially agree with something.

52
00:04:18,500 --> 00:04:22,900
For example, a speaker may shift their opinion during a conversation, or

53
00:04:22,900 --> 00:04:26,060
correct a misunderstanding that another speaker has.

54
00:04:26,400 --> 00:04:30,680
In the IELTS listening, these are called distractors, and tracking multiple

55
00:04:30,680 --> 00:04:34,160
speakers to avoid distractors is a very important skill.

56
00:04:34,880 --> 00:04:38,220
And finally, challenge four, the pace.

57
00:04:38,970 --> 00:04:43,600
Section 3 conversations tend to move faster than Parts 1 and 2.

58
00:04:43,880 --> 00:04:48,340
Speakers are engaged, excited about their topic, and they pack a lot of

59
00:04:48,340 --> 00:04:50,260
information into a short time.

60
00:04:55,160 --> 00:04:58,600
So, how can we tackle the challenging Section 3?

61
00:04:58,800 --> 00:05:01,060
Don't worry, because I am here to help.

62
00:05:01,340 --> 00:05:06,160
Section 3 discussions aren't random, but they follow predictable patterns.

63
00:05:06,420 --> 00:05:09,440
Understanding the flow gives you a huge advantage.

64
00:05:09,440 --> 00:05:13,500
Most Section 3 conversations follow a structure like this.

65
00:05:14,100 --> 00:05:18,100
Notice how questions in Section 3 are designed to follow this structure.

66
00:05:18,680 --> 00:05:25,360
Questions 21 to 23 might be about the first topic, 24 to 26 about the second,

67
00:05:25,540 --> 00:05:26,320
and so on.

68
00:05:26,920 --> 00:05:33,720
This is why reading the questions before listening is so crucial, as it helps

69
00:05:33,720 --> 00:05:36,080
you predict the conversation's path.

70
00:05:36,630 --> 00:05:40,240
Strategy 2, look out for a signposting language.

71
00:05:40,700 --> 00:05:46,360
Think of these as verbal road signs that tell
you exactly where the conversation is going.

72
00:05:46,600 --> 00:05:51,500
Let me introduce you to the main categories
of signposting you'll hear in Part 3.

73
00:05:52,100 --> 00:05:54,380
Category 1, agreement signals.

74
00:05:54,580 --> 00:05:58,480
When you hear phrases like, that's a good point, I completely agree.

75
00:05:59,000 --> 00:06:01,760
Or exactly, and what's more.

76
00:06:02,260 --> 00:06:04,720
The speaker is building on the previous idea.

77
00:06:05,040 --> 00:06:07,920
The answer may continue in the same direction.

78
00:06:08,760 --> 00:06:13,820
Phrases like, however, you know, but don't you think, on the other hand, or

79
00:06:13,820 --> 00:06:17,280
I'm not so sure about that, tell you an opposing view is coming.

80
00:06:17,540 --> 00:06:20,440
This is often where the correct answer hides.

81
00:06:20,940 --> 00:06:23,240
Category 3, topic change signals.

82
00:06:23,560 --> 00:06:27,100
Moving on to another aspect we should consider.

83
00:06:27,620 --> 00:06:29,660
Let's talk about, what about.

84
00:06:30,380 --> 00:06:33,540
These signal you're entering new question territory.

85
00:06:33,540 --> 00:06:36,000
Look down at your next set of questions.

86
00:06:36,700 --> 00:06:40,220
Category 4, example and clarification signals.

87
00:06:40,720 --> 00:06:46,220
For instance, take the case of, what I mean is, to put it another way.

88
00:06:46,800 --> 00:06:51,600
An example or explanation is coming, which often contains the specific detail

89
00:06:51,600 --> 00:06:52,820
you need for the answer.

90
00:06:53,740 --> 00:06:57,320
Category 5, conclusion and summary signals.

91
00:06:58,250 --> 00:07:03,693
So basically, in conclusion, the main point is

92
00:07:03,717 --> 00:07:09,160
what it comes down to is, pay special attention.

93
00:07:09,780 --> 00:07:12,600
This often summarizes the key point you need.

94
00:07:13,180 --> 00:07:16,000
Strategy 3, tracking the speakers.

95
00:07:16,540 --> 00:07:21,800
Within the first few seconds of the audio, you need to identify the speakers.

96
00:07:21,800 --> 00:07:27,000
The recording almost always introduces the speakers by name at the beginning.

97
00:07:27,340 --> 00:07:32,980
You might hear something like, so, Sarah and
Tom, let's discuss your research project.

98
00:07:33,520 --> 00:07:36,780
Or Professor Williams, we wanted to ask you about.

99
00:07:37,300 --> 00:07:41,000
Write these names down quickly in the margin of your question paper.

100
00:07:41,580 --> 00:07:45,720
Understanding who each person is helps you predict what they might say.

101
00:07:45,940 --> 00:07:49,360
In Section 3, you'll typically encounter these roles.

102
00:07:49,360 --> 00:07:51,820
First, the tutor or professor.

103
00:07:52,180 --> 00:07:56,900
This person usually guides the discussion, asks probing questions, offers

104
00:07:56,900 --> 00:07:59,620
expert opinions, and summarizes key points.

105
00:08:00,340 --> 00:08:02,720
Their voice often sounds more authoritative and measured.

106
00:08:03,620 --> 00:08:05,000
Second, the students.

107
00:08:05,260 --> 00:08:09,880
They share their findings, express opinions that may change, ask questions, and

108
00:08:09,880 --> 00:08:10,960
respond to feedback.

109
00:08:11,240 --> 00:08:12,160
Why does this matter?

110
00:08:12,340 --> 00:08:16,560
Because questions often ask specifically about what one person thinks.

111
00:08:16,560 --> 00:08:20,940
If you hear, what does the tutor suggest about the research methodology?

112
00:08:21,740 --> 00:08:26,880
You need to filter out what the student
said and focus only on the tutor's words.

113
00:08:27,680 --> 00:08:33,340
In Section 3, different speakers often have
different opinions about the same topic.

114
00:08:33,539 --> 00:08:38,299
Your questions might ask specifically whose opinion something is.

115
00:08:38,500 --> 00:08:43,039
Academic discussions have natural turn-taking patterns, but they also have

116
00:08:43,039 --> 00:08:45,160
interruptions and quick exchanges.

117
00:08:45,900 --> 00:08:49,340
Train yourself to notice these switch signals.

118
00:08:50,320 --> 00:08:53,140
Direct address, when someone uses a name.

119
00:08:53,400 --> 00:08:55,360
For example, what do you think, Sarah?

120
00:08:55,620 --> 00:08:57,500
Now you know Sarah will speak next.

121
00:08:57,980 --> 00:08:59,980
Agreement or disagreement phrases.

122
00:09:00,420 --> 00:09:04,760
For example, I agree with Tom tells you the current speaker is not Tom.

123
00:09:05,160 --> 00:09:11,520
Finally, questions from the tutor or the
professor often redirect to another speaker.

124
00:09:11,520 --> 00:09:14,760
Like, and how about you Tom, do you see it the same way?

125
00:09:15,160 --> 00:09:19,460
Finally, the last strategy is don't panic at missed answers.

126
00:09:19,740 --> 00:09:21,780
If you miss an answer, let it go.

127
00:09:22,040 --> 00:09:26,220
Write your best guess and immediately refocus on the next question.

128
00:09:26,460 --> 00:09:29,660
One missed question should not cost you three or four more.

129
00:09:29,880 --> 00:09:32,240
The audio keeps moving, and so must you.

130
00:09:32,860 --> 00:09:35,080
Now let's review our lesson today through some exercises.

131
00:09:40,450 --> 00:09:43,250
Let's quickly recap what we've covered today.

132
00:09:43,250 --> 00:09:48,150
Section 3 is an academic discussion with two to four speakers.

133
00:09:48,590 --> 00:09:54,050
It's challenging because of multiple voices, abstract ideas, changing opinions,

134
00:09:54,410 --> 00:09:56,850
heavy paraphrasing, and faster pacing.

135
00:09:57,290 --> 00:10:00,450
We have the strategies for Section 3 as follow.

136
00:10:00,850 --> 00:10:03,930
Strategy 1, understand the conversation structure.

137
00:10:04,490 --> 00:10:08,050
Strategy 2, listen for signposting language.

138
00:10:08,530 --> 00:10:11,290
Strategy 3, track multiple speakers.

139
00:10:11,290 --> 00:10:15,050
Strategy 4, don't panic at missed answers.

140
00:10:15,470 --> 00:10:18,570
In our next lesson, we'll put this knowledge into practice.

141
00:10:18,710 --> 00:10:20,490
Goodbye, and see you in the next lesson.

